<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lesotho</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kingdom of Lesotho, Basutoland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lesotho, officially the Kingdom of Lesotho, is a landlocked country in Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, entirely surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. It emerged as a single polity, Basutoland, under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a> in 1822, whose followers had joined to resist the Lifaqane. Encroaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> settlers and wars with them cost the kingdom its western lowlands, and Moshoeshoe appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, who made Basutoland a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1868. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a> in 1884 and gained independence from the United Kingdom on 4 October 1966, as the Kingdom of Lesotho.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lesotho covers 30,355 square kilometres in the Maloti Mountains and is the only independent state in the world that lies entirely above 1,000 metres. Its lowest point, at 1,400 metres, is the highest lowest point of any country, and over 80 per cent of the land lies above 1,800 metres. It is the southernmost landlocked country in the world. Because of its elevation it stays cooler than other regions of the same latitude, and snow is common in the highlands between May and September. The western lowlands form the main agricultural zone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a> was a son of Mokhachane, a minor chief of the Bakoteli lineage, and became a chief around 1804. Between 1820 and 1823 he and his followers settled at the Butha-Buthe Mountain, joining with former adversaries to resist the Lifaqane, the upheavals associated with the reign of Shaka kaSenzangakhona from 1818 to 1828. The state developed further out of conflicts between British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonists leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, and out of the Orange River Sovereignty and the Orange Free State that followed.</p><p>Missionaries Thomas Arbousset, Eugène Casalis and Constant Gosselin of the Paris Evangelical Missionary Society, invited by Moshoeshoe, were placed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morija">Morija</a>. Between 1837 and 1855 they developed Sesotho orthography and printed works in the language. Casalis acted as translator and adviser on foreign affairs and helped acquire guns for use against the encroaching Europeans and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua people</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basotho-national-party">Basotho National Party</a> ruled from independence in 1966 until January 1970, and a de facto government led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leabua-jonathan">Leabua Jonathan</a> followed. Constitutional government was restored in 1993 after seven years of military rule. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-ii">Moshoeshoe II</a> was exiled in 1990, returned in 1992 and was reinstated in 1995; he died the next year and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/letsie-iii">Letsie III</a> took the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maseru">Maseru</a>. The Sotho, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a>, from whom the country takes its name, make up about 99.7 per cent of the population of roughly 2.3 million, and Sesotho is an official language alongside English. Lesotho is a parliamentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> in which the king is head of state with a largely ceremonial function. It is divided into ten districts, and its national dress is built around the Basotho blanket.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lesotho” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its wars with the Basotho had lasting results: the land won west of the Caledon River, known as the Conquered Territory, remained part of the Free State, while Moshoeshoe’s appeal to Britain led to the protectorate that became modern <b>Lesotho</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Swazi Kingdom is one of three African monarchies that survived the colonial era as sovereign states, together with <b>Lesotho</b> and Morocco, and the only one that remains an absolute monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
